1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,700
In today's lecture, I'm going to be talking about microplastics.

2
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:12,770
Microplastics are tiny pieces of plastic smaller than five millimetres in size.

3
00:00:12,770 --> 00:00:18,799
Recently there's been a greater awareness that there are large quantities of plastic waste -

4
00:00:18,799 --> 00:00:21,210
big and small - in the environment.

5
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:27,343
The amount of plastic waste in the oceans has received widespread attention,

6
00:00:27,343 --> 00:00:35,520
but far less is known about the effects of microplastics in freshwater and particularly in soil.

7
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:41,270
Microplastics can enter the environment via a number of different sources.

8
00:00:41,270 --> 00:00:48,547
Threads and microfibres detach from synthetic clothing every time they're put in a washing machine,

9
00:00:48,547 --> 00:00:52,640
and these find their way into the water system.

10
00:00:52,640 --> 00:00:59,285
Other sources include big pieces of plastic waste that are already in the environment,

11
00:00:59,285 --> 00:01:04,980
and these break down into microscopic particles over a period of time.

12
00:01:04,980 --> 00:01:09,451
On a larger scale, factory waste is another route,

13
00:01:09,451 --> 00:01:17,400
as are tyres which wear down as cars, lorries and so on travel along road surfaces.

14
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:25,330
We already understand some of the impacts of microplastics from studies involving fish and other animals.

15
00:01:25,330 --> 00:01:32,137
There is evidence that microplastics harm small creatures in a variety of ways,

16
00:01:32,137 --> 00:01:35,279
such as by damaging their mouths,

17
00:01:35,279 --> 00:01:37,000
or by impairing their ability to feed

18
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:44,180
for example when microplastics get lodged in their digestive system.

19
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:47,619
Surprisingly perhaps, it is likely that humans consume microplastics,

20
00:01:47,619 --> 00:01:52,970
as these have been detected in a wide range of food and drink products,

21
00:01:52,970 --> 00:01:58,320
including bottled water, as well as in water that comes direct from the tap.

22
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:06,540
What's more, salt and many kinds of seafood have also been found to contain microplastics.

23
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:11,240
However, it's important to underline that there is not

24
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:15,940
yet conclusive proof that microplastics cause significant harm to people.

25
00:02:15,940 --> 00:02:22,012
In many countries, including here in the UK, there is legislation

26
00:02:22,012 --> 00:02:29,740
which prevents manufacturers from adding plastic microbeads to shower gels, facial cleansers and toothpaste.

27
00:02:29,740 --> 00:02:38,451
It is very difficult to accurately estimate the total amount of microplastic particles in the soil as they can be hard to detect,

28
00:02:38,451 --> 00:02:44,890
but we do know they are carried in the air and deposited in the soil by rain.

29
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:55,410
What's more, many of the fertilisers used by both farmers and gardeners contain microplastics.

30
00:02:55,410 --> 00:03:05,790
A team from the Anglia Ruskin University in Cambridge has carried out a study of the effects of microplastics on the digestive tracts of earthworms.

31
00:03:05,790 --> 00:03:08,160
These worms, which live in topsoil

32
00:03:08,240 --> 00:03:13,530
are an essential component of our agricultural system.

33
00:03:13,530 --> 00:03:21,130
By feeding on soil, they mix nutrients into it, thereby making it more fertile.

34
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:23,160
The researchers set out to discover

35
00:03:23,160 --> 00:03:27,412
whether the introduction of microplastics into the soil-

36
00:03:27,412 --> 00:03:31,002
and the subsequent ingestion of these by earthworms -

37
00:03:31,002 --> 00:03:35,040
would impact soil quality and ultimately inhibit plant growth.

38
00:03:35,040 --> 00:03:37,570
The short answer was, yes, it did.

39
00:03:37,570 --> 00:03:41,920
After placing three different types of microplastic particles into the soil

40
00:03:42,060 --> 00:03:45,670
they planted perennial rye grass.

41
00:03:45,670 --> 00:03:52,009
The particles of microplastic, which included biodegradable PLA and conventional high-density polyethylene,

42
00:03:52,009 --> 00:03:57,820
or HDPE, were then ingested by the earthworms in the soil.

43
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:01,290
The result was that the worms lost weight rapidly.

44
00:04:01,290 --> 00:04:06,632
What's more, a lower percentage than normal of the rye grass seeds germinated,

45
00:04:06,632 --> 00:04:16,170
and the researchers concluded that this was a direct result of the earthworms being unable to fulfil their normal role in making soil more fertile.

46
00:04:16,170 --> 00:04:24,073
The team also discovered that there was an increase in the amount of acid found in the soil,

47
00:04:24,073 --> 00:04:29,780
and this was attributed mainly to the microplastic particles from conventional HDPE plastic.

48
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:33,239
The conclusions of the study make for very interesting reading -

49
00:04:33,239 --> 00:04:39,120
I've included the reference in the notes to give you at the end of this session.

50
00:04:39,120 --> 00:04:47,810
To summarise, the authors proposed the idea that we need to regard soil as we would regard any other process in nature.

51
00:04:47,810 --> 00:04:53,156
This means we should accept the implications of soil being dependent on decaying

52
00:04:53,156 --> 00:04:57,680
and dead matter constantly being passed through the bodies of earthworms.

53
00:04:57,680 --> 00:05:02,754
That is, when soil becomes impoverished by the presence of microplastics,

54
00:05:02,754 --> 00:05:08,290
not only ecosystems but also the whole of society are negatively impacted.
